# Docker deployment environment (production Compose).
# Copy to .env.deploy and fill the required secrets:
#   node scripts/generate-env.mjs   # creates .env.deploy + fills secrets
# Then run the stack explicitly against it:
#   docker compose --env-file .env.deploy -f docker-compose.yml up -d
# (Passing --env-file replaces the default .env as Compose's interpolation
# file — the dev .env is never read for deployment, though host shell
# environment variables can still override individual values. Conversely, no
# dev tooling (pnpm dev / vite / drizzle / vitest) ever reads this file.)
# Source-build verification (contributors / PR acceptance) merges the build
# override, which replaces the prebuilt image with THIS checkout:
#   docker compose --env-file .env.deploy \
#     -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.build.yml up -d --build

# ── Required secrets (no defaults; generate-env fills, never rotates) ────
JWT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Topology ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=exam
POSTGRES_DB=exam

# Prebuilt production image, pinned to the release version. generate-env
# derives it from .release-version (ghcr.io/jnhu76/exam:vX.Y.Z); an explicit
# value always wins. Offline installs: pull on a connected machine,
# `docker save`, transfer, `docker load`, keep this same reference.
EXAM_IMAGE=

# Host port publishing the app container (container-internal port stays
# 3000). CORS_ORIGIN / PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN default to
# http://localhost:<EXAM_PORT>; set them to your machine's address for LAN.
# EXAM_PORT=3000
# CORS_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.5:3000
# PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.5:3000

# APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Shanghai
# TZ=Asia/Shanghai

# ── Optional: trusted reverse proxy (HTTPS deployments) ──────────────────
# CIDRs whose sockets may rewrite the client IP via X-Forwarded-For
# (comma-separated; a bare IP means that one host). Unset = trust nobody:
# request.ip stays the socket address and forwarding headers are ignored.
# PRECONDITION: cover ONLY the proxy→API link — NEVER the candidate client
# network (and never a client LAN CIDR): a trusted CIDR that covers clients
# lets them forge their rate-limit/audit identity. Trust-all CIDRs
# (0.0.0.0/0, ::/0) are rejected at startup. Behind a reverse proxy, set
# this to the proxy's addresses AND configure the proxy to append the real
# client IP (nginx: proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For
# $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;) — see the deployment runbook §2
# "Rate-limit identity, trusted proxies, and sizing".
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=127.0.0.1/32

# ── Optional: Redis (enable with `--profile redis`) ──────────────────────
# REDIS_PASSWORD=<secret>
# REDIS_URL=redis://:<secret>@redis:6379

# ── Optional: email (defaults: disabled, fake transport — nothing sent) ──
# EMAIL_ENABLED=true
# EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.your-org.internal
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=

# ── Optional: first-install launchpad token ──────────────────────────────
# LAUNCHPAD_SETUP_TOKEN=
